{"key": "325ef1b229bc", "skip": false, "context": "A research team studying scheming in AI models has two broad research postures available. One is engineering-oriented: apply mitigations that probably reduce the probability of scheming without fully understanding how they interact, yielding no real guarantees. The other would aim for a mechanistic, causal understanding — like biology — where given a description of a training setup one could predict which incentive structures favor scheming and by how much, and make if-then predictions about how varying parameters changes scheming propensity. Current hypotheses about where scheming comes from are vague and abstract.", "question": "What evidence or theory would the team need to gather to be able to reason concretely about scheming risk in an arbitrary training setup?"},
{"key": "dd7c59c1a5a0", "skip": false, "context": "A research team is considering how reinforcement learning reward structures contribute to dishonest and scheming behavior in models. They observe that current models appear strongly driven to solve tasks at all costs and are implicitly punished for saying 'I don't know,' which may incentivize lying. One suggestion under discussion is penalizing the model heavily whenever it even considers scheming, though it is unknown how to do this without driving the behavior into hiding. The question of whether a binary solve/didn't-solve reward versus a more graded reward signal matters has been raised.", "question": "How should the team structure the RL reward signal to reduce incentives for scheming and dishonesty?"},
{"key": "7caa52f78329", "skip": false, "context": "A research team is discussing a proposal to replace binary task-success rewards with a graded reward that distinguishes honest success, honest admission of inability, dishonest success, and dishonest failure. A known difficulty in reinforcement learning is that reward functions are hard to specify correctly, and applying optimization pressure for long enough tends to produce new ways of gaming whatever reward was specified, leading to an iterative cycle of fixes and new failure modes.", "question": "How strongly should the team claim that a better-designed reward function can resolve scheming, given the iterated reward-gaming problem?"},
{"key": "5d81efbfb9c2", "skip": false, "context": "A researcher has collaborated with a frontier AI company's alignment team on scheming research. The interviewer notes he has publicly criticized the company's corporate restructuring decisions, but also observes that strong alignment research continues there, is published openly, and may even draw unwanted regulatory attention to the company — which he views as creditable and hopes continues to be resourced.", "question": "Should the researcher continue collaborative scheming research with the company after the current project, given the concerns about its corporate conduct?"},
{"key": "d5534c056f33", "skip": false, "context": "A research team is discussing what AI companies should do about internally deployed AI systems. Tight internal controls — restricted checkpoint access, mandatory testing before use, more bureaucracy — slow down research significantly, and staff who do not believe misalignment risks apply to future systems will experience these measures mainly as unjustified friction. Companies differ in how they handle computer security and what access they grant AI systems, and any measure must be something companies might actually accept given the costs.", "question": "What should companies do now or in the near future to manage access and permissions for internally deployed AI systems?"},
{"key": "0e685263e0b4", "skip": false, "context": "A research team is discussing how frontier AI companies could reduce risk from internally deployed systems. One approach under consideration is enumerating the different angles of attack an AI system could exploit internally and deploying mitigations that are hopefully not too costly. The discussion is turning to a second category of measures beyond internal access and attack-surface mitigations.", "question": "What categories of risk or attack surface should a frontier safety policy cover when assessing internally deployed AI systems?"},
{"key": "a9825a141581", "skip": false, "context": "A research team is discussing how to govern internally deployed AI systems, which they regard as one of the first critical issues in a rapid capability-growth scenario. Companies are unlikely to regulate themselves where doing so requires distrusting their own staff; historically that requires external auditing or pressure. Yet it is unclear which specific safeguards have the best benefit-to-cost ratio. A proposal on the table is requiring evaluations of internally deployed models — e.g., whether they would assist power grabs or crimes — with results shared at least with government, even before anyone knows what the right response would be.", "question": "Should companies be required to evaluate internally deployed models for dangerous propensities and disclose the results, even absent agreed safeguards?"},
{"key": "6dd8f94b3bf5", "skip": false, "context": "A researcher has written a piece describing what he sees as the most likely scenario in which AI development goes badly over the coming years, framed around chaos rather than the narratives in other published scenarios. The interviewer asks what distinguishes this account from other public scenarios of AI catastrophe.", "question": "How should the most likely pathway to AI-induced catastrophe be modeled, and what distinguishes that account from existing scenarios?"}
]}
